From: jkastellec-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org (John Kastellec)
Date: Mon, 07 Jul 2008 21:40:09 +0700
Subject: Question about nearest neighbor matching with and without replacement
Message-ID: <48722AC9.60203@gmail.com>






Hello,




My understanding is that using replacement with nearest neighbor
matching results in fewer options being unmatched, since control (or
treatment) observations can be used more than once.  However, I've
compared the results of running "MatchIt" commands using both replace =
TRUE and replace == FALSE on several datasets, and it seems that using
replacement always results in more observations being
unmatched. 



Here, for example are the results from the Carpenter dataset analyzed
in the Ho et. al PA article (2007):



Without replacement:



Call: 

matchit(formula = spec, data = data, method = "nearest", discard =
"both", 

    replace = FALSE, exact = c("acutediz", "peddiz01", "hosp01", 

        "lethal", "femdiz01", "mandiz01"))



Sample sizes:

          Control Treated

All           245     163

Matched       153     153

Unmatched      77       8

Discarded      15       2



With Replacement



Call: 

matchit(formula = spec, data = data, method = "nearest", discard =
"both", 

    replace = TRUE, exact = c("acutediz", "peddiz01", "hosp01", 

        "lethal", "femdiz01", "mandiz01"))



Sample sizes:

          Control Treated

All           245     163

Matched        90     161

Unmatched     140       0

Discarded      15       2



Am I missing something?



Thanks very much,



John

From estuart-SZLOfyx5T3qHXe+LvDLADg at public.gmane.org  Mon Jul  7 15:45:38 2008
From: estuart-SZLOfyx5T3qHXe+LvDLADg at public.gmane.org (Elizabeth Stuart)
Date: Mon, 07 Jul 2008 11:45:38 -0400
Subject: Re: Question about nearest neighbor matching with and without replacement
In-Reply-To: <48722AC9.60203@gmail.com>
References: <48722AC9.60203@gmail.com>
Message-ID: <C497B262.DE63%estuart@jhsph.edu>

Hi John,
Matching with replacement is typically implemented (as it is in MatchIt) to
use controls more than once, but treated individuals are still only matched
once (i.e., with 1:1 matching each treated individual gets 1 match, and the
controls may be used as matches multiple times).

So matching with replacement will always result in more control observations
being unmatched because fewer of them get usedwhen matching without
replacement more controls need to be used, but when matching with
replacement some controls are used multiple times so fewer unique controls
are used.

In your case the matching with replacement leads to more treated being
matched because of your exact restrictions...when matching with replacement
all of the treated individuals can get an exact match, but when matching
without replacement that isn¹t possible.  If you didn¹t have the exact
restrictions you would find that the number of treated matched would be the
same with and without replacement.

I hope this helps.

Liz

