Hi Andrew,
There is not an automated way to get that sample sizes matrix, but it is
fairly easy to get all of the information yourself.

First, to get the overall sample sizes (number matched, unmatched, etc.),
you can do the following (using the ³lalonde² data as an example):

> temp <- matchit(treat ~ age+educ+married, data=lalonde, method="exact")
> m.data <- match.data(temp)
# The number of treated and control in the original data
> table(lalonde$treat)

  0   1 
429 185 
# The number of treated and control in the matched data
> table(m.data$treat)

  0   1 
131  91 

If you want the sample sizes shown by subclass and treatment/control status,
it is just the following:
> summary(temp)$q.table
   Treated Control Total
1        5       3     8
2        1       1     2
3        2       2     4
4        3       4     7
5        1       3     4
6        1       2     3
7        1       1     2
8        1       2     3
9        4       3     7
10       3       1     4
11       1       2     3
12       1       2     3
13       3       6     9
14       1       1     2
15       2       1     3
16       5      11    16
17       3       5     8
...

Liz


